        "target": "Given the problem, I identified the key quantities: a clerk can process 25 forms per hour, and 2400 forms must be processed in an 8-hour day. The unknown I needed to find was the number of clerks required.\n\nFirst, I calculated the number of forms one clerk can process in an 8-hour day:\n\\[ 25 \\text{ forms/hour} \\times 8 \\text{ hours} = 200 \\text{ forms/day} \\]\n\nNext, I determined how many clerks are needed to process 2400 forms:\n\\[ \\frac{2400 \\text{ forms}}{200 \\text{ forms/clerk}} = 12 \\text{ clerks} \\]\n\nTo verify this, I checked if 12 clerks, each processing 200 forms per day, would indeed process 2400 forms:\n\\[ 12 \\text{ clerks} \\times 200 \\text{ forms/clerk} = 2400 \\text{ forms} \\]\n\nThis calculation matched the required number of forms, confirming my solution.\n\nThe operation I would reuse is the calculation of the number of forms one clerk can process in a day, and then using that to determine the number of clerks needed. This is a reusable operation because it can be applied to similar problems where the processing rate and total forms to be processed are given.\n\nFINAL: 12",
        "target_sha256": "814330e404e35a184a37fbe1857225304c8f674b73d5b3ae5bc600b7761c2778"
